  "worked_solution": "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(2x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=0.$",
